  "vegTitle": "Blue Gum & Gum-topped Box Woodland",
  "vegSub": "Regional Ecosystems 12.3.3 / 12.11.18 / 12.9-10.3 · Queensland Blue Gum woodland on alluvial plains, with Gum-topped Box forest on the rises (12.3.3 Endangered) · South-east Queensland",
  "intro": "Before 1750 your address at Gin Gin stood in tall Queensland Blue Gum woodland on the creek flats and alluvial plains, rising into Gum-topped Box and ironbark forest on the low hills around it. The gums grew in stands from dense forest to open parkland over a rich, grassy floor of Kangaroo Grass, bluegrasses and wildflowers, with few shrubs. The Blue Gum woodland is one of the most heavily cleared communities in South-east Queensland and is now listed as Endangered; it is koala food country, and its old hollow-bearing trees shelter gliders, bats and birds. The plants below are indigenous to these communities and grouped by the layer they fill. Plant the canopy first; the grasses, lilies and herbs belong in the open light beneath it.",
